[[:db/retract 17592186159266 :answer-field/choices 17592186159267] [:db/retract 17592186159266 :answer-field/choices 17592186159268] [:db/retract 17592186159266 :answer-field/choices 17592186159269] [:db/retract 17592186159266 :answer-field/choices 17592186159270] [:db/retract 17592186159266 :answer-field/choices 17592186159271] [:db/retract 17592186162348 :question/answer-fields 17592186159290] {:db/id 17592186161903 :question/problem "For the matrix \n![](images/1d/1d0733f2c724253c804c596e1d0a1432b8281afa7a14a9dc6702b099ab4795e0.svg) find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162089 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3fcee73d-521b-5fd0-a9c6-c7b6d8e05cce"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161902 :question/problem "![](images/39/399738d7c4da59c73c954cfd41d8c76fc93ad0351fc8f2854ad33796aa2379df.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U \\Sigma V ^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma.$ \n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/f6/f6703dcf594334ec5ab821bbaac5654de5e3970b0e7981f256a8fd00df31060f.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161904 :question/problem "![](images/fd/fdc735895573e0d7c1eb7865233dd7b56a38aeeb9c76bba77fa669212262791d.svg)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse $A^+.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r ^T$ is a reduced singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV_r \\, \\Sigma_r^+ \\, U_r^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Sigma_r^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma_r.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n![](images/d7/d750c1c5030e0d1df7e8b14790e3f189e69346ca392d3d2885ad49db0e66b7e0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={V}_{r}\\,{Σ}_{r}^{+}\\,{U}_{r}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{50}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 16 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 12 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162344 :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159266 :answer-field/correct "answer-065fffc8-3e59-5969-929b-5087533bf93f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-597aa326-f1e8-5701-9c20-5031aee7b048" "answer-39695dbd-9cb4-567f-a279-8277626c4d7e" "answer-fcd31e64-a634-576e-a8e8-c7e2bca6ed34" "answer-ffb3d5ad-4c33-5033-8c68-0522a4f523a1" "answer-065fffc8-3e59-5969-929b-5087533bf93f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-597aa326-f1e8-5701-9c20-5031aee7b048" :answer/id #uuid "597aa326-f1e8-5701-9c20-5031aee7b048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39695dbd-9cb4-567f-a279-8277626c4d7e" :answer/id #uuid "39695dbd-9cb4-567f-a279-8277626c4d7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcd31e64-a634-576e-a8e8-c7e2bca6ed34" :answer/id #uuid "fcd31e64-a634-576e-a8e8-c7e2bca6ed34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffb3d5ad-4c33-5033-8c68-0522a4f523a1" :answer/id #uuid "ffb3d5ad-4c33-5033-8c68-0522a4f523a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-065fffc8-3e59-5969-929b-5087533bf93f" :answer/id #uuid "065fffc8-3e59-5969-929b-5087533bf93f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{25}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3fcee73d-521b-5fd0-a9c6-c7b6d8e05cce" :question/id #uuid "3fcee73d-521b-5fd0-a9c6-c7b6d8e05cce" :question/math-academy-id "q-138516" :question/problem "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ find the pseudoinverse ${A}^{+}.$" :question/worked-solution "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nV\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere ${Σ}^{+}$ is the pseudoinverse of $Σ.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{Σ}^{+}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{U}^{T}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & =V\\,{Σ}^{+}\\,{U}^{T} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2881387b-754e-56f3-834a-ac7d858e2ecf"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2881387b-754e-56f3-834a-ac7d858e2ecf" :answer-field/id #uuid "2881387b-754e-56f3-834a-ac7d858e2ecf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8e69fde6-984a-5a8b-8dd0-63b58423f006" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c0338917-af25-5f02-ba7d-b2159df253f6" "answer-8e69fde6-984a-5a8b-8dd0-63b58423f006" "answer-afeacb94-4e5b-58cc-be9f-c0b7884171f0" "answer-3fe77835-4c02-5a54-960e-f2c670d5fae4" "answer-38c000fb-0f6e-5949-8db6-c4c5d28b3248"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c0338917-af25-5f02-ba7d-b2159df253f6" :answer/id #uuid "c0338917-af25-5f02-ba7d-b2159df253f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e69fde6-984a-5a8b-8dd0-63b58423f006" :answer/id #uuid "8e69fde6-984a-5a8b-8dd0-63b58423f006" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afeacb94-4e5b-58cc-be9f-c0b7884171f0" :answer/id #uuid "afeacb94-4e5b-58cc-be9f-c0b7884171f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fe77835-4c02-5a54-960e-f2c670d5fae4" :answer/id #uuid "3fe77835-4c02-5a54-960e-f2c670d5fae4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38c000fb-0f6e-5949-8db6-c4c5d28b3248" :answer/id #uuid "38c000fb-0f6e-5949-8db6-c4c5d28b3248" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163306 :tutorial/content "The invertible matrix theorem tells us that if $A$ is an $n \\times n$ matrix with full rank (i.e., $\\mathrm{rank}(A)=n$), then there exists a unique inverse matrix $A^{-1}$ such that \n\n$$\nAA^{-1}=A^{-1}\\!A=I_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $I_n$ denotes the $n \\times n$ identity matrix.\n\nThere are two questions that we can ask:\n\n- Can we extend the notion of an inverse matrix to one that works for $n\\times n$ matrices $A$ where $\\mathrm{rank}(A) < n?$\n\n- Is it possible to generalize the concept of an inverse to non-square matrices?\n\nThe answer to both questions is yes! This generalization of the inverse of $A$ is called the **pseudoinverse** of $A,$ and is denoted $A^+.$ The dimensions of $A^+$ are the same as $A^T.$\n\nAs an example, let's consider the ${\\color{red}{3}}\\times {\\color{blue}{2}}$ matrix $A$ and its corresponding pseudoinverse, given below:\n\n![](images/87/87581a69d3d76dbf3b0ebca8941c9a82b5b9443b00e86f32e4e9e4a42ef5e18b.svg)\n\n- Computing $A^{+}\\!A,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+}\\,A & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is the identity matrix $I_{\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\n- Computing $A\\!A^{+},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{+} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *almost* the identity matrix $I_{\\color{red}{3}}.$\n\nWe can explain this result using the following properties of the pseudoinverse:\n\n- *If the columns of an $m\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $A^+A = I_{\\color{blue}{n}}.$*\n\n- *If the rows of an ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times n$ matrix $A$ are linearly independent, then $AA^+ = I_{\\color{red}{m}}.$*\n\nIn the example above, the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, but the rows are not.\n\nMoreover, the notion of a pseudoinverse is consistent with inverse matrices. In particular,\n\n*If $A$ is an invertible square matrix, then $A^{+} = A^{-1}.$*\n\nThis lesson will discuss how to compute the pseudoinverse for any matrix, starting with an important special case." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163314 :tutorial/content "For an *almost* zero ${\\color{red}{m}}\\times {\\color{blue}{n}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n\\sigma_1, \\: \\sigma_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: \\sigma_r\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only, the corresponding pseudoinverse is the *almost* zero ${\\color{blue}{n}}\\times {\\color{red}{m}}$ matrix with non-zero entries \n\n$$\n{\\sigma_1^{-1}}, \\, {\\sigma_2^{-1}}, \\, \\ldots, \\, {\\sigma_r^{-1}}\n$$\n\non the main diagonal only.\n\nFor example, for the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/d6/d6d6a167747f99c5a48be59ae3e11ca7b5a9188e80ddeb63b8c0f193d991b18c.svg)\n\nwe calculate its pseudoinverse as follows:\n\n- **Step 1:** Replace the non-zero elements on the main diagonal with their multiplicative inverses.\n\n- **Step 2:** Transpose the resulting matrix.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{+} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{+} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{3}^{-1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {4}^{-1} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1/3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1/4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163313 :tutorial/content "There are many ways to find the pseudoinverse of a given matrix $A.$ One way is to use the singular value decomposition of $A{:}$\n\nSpecifically, if \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V ^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A,$ then the pseudoinverse of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\nA^+ = V \\, \\Sigma^+ \\, U^T,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\Sigma^+$ is the pseudoinverse of $\\Sigma.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163307 :tutorial/content "Consider the matrix\n\n![](images/98/987aa533ca51a9f101ba590b7ade0753983ae219534342aad42054316b2e02ec.svg)\n\nand its singular value decomposition $A=U \\Sigma V ^T,$ where\n\n![](images/69/69e68ac94c6e25b2609f7d20616d7d564a1225abad748c3e4fd94516cb6df72f.svg)\n\nNotice that $\\Sigma$ contains a row of zeros. In these situations, it's possible to simplify the decomposition of $A.$ This simplified decomposition is called the **reduced singular value decomposition** of $A.$\n\nFirst, we note that $A$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. In addition, the matrix $\\Sigma$ has two non-zero singular values on its main diagonal, so the rank of $A$ is $\\color{blue}r=2.$ \n\nTo find our reduced SVD, we proceed as follows:\n\n- First, we remove all zero rows and columns from $\\Sigma.$ In our example, we should delete the $3$rd row since it contains only zeros. Notice that we obtain a square invertible matrix of rank ${\\color{blue}2}{:}$ \n![](images/a5/a56440b8808003f25bec4b61d4d06af7274a5eea68da4e94af9e91af8d64d02a.svg)\n\n- Next, we remove all extra columns of $U$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should delete the $3$rd column:\n![](images/e4/e4ba93ef3588affc421af821cb9de1051749396c69a9e64523399f7fc4e3bc5b.svg)\n\n- Finally, we remove all extra columns of $V$ starting from the right, keeping only $\\color{blue}2$ columns. In our example, we should keep $V$ as it is since it already has only $2$ columns: \n![](images/b1/b146b37075dda593ded5b86d58e6cfba3de463e01727b39756a6b90cbb88a95f.svg)\n\nThe reduced singular value decomposition of $A$ is then given by\n\n$$\nA = U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T.\n$$\n\nSo, in our example, we have\n\n![](images/c1/c1eed35f9b7aa3214e9783f8f80952e57adca6c4d4b81efaeb49255b5973f3af.svg)\n\nIn general, a singular value decomposition \n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\n \n\nof an $m \\times n$ matrix $A$ is not unique. However, we can always simplify it to the corresponding reduced singular value decomposition \n\n$$\nA=U_r \\Sigma_r V_r^T\n$$\n\n \n\nwith the following properties:\n\n- $\\Sigma_r$ is a square diagonal matrix of size $r\\times r,$ where $r\\leq \\min\\{m,n\\}$ is the rank of $A.$ The main diagonal of $\\Sigma_r$ contains the non-zero singular values $\\sigma_1, \\sigma_2, \\ldots, \\sigma_r$ of $A$ in descending order.\n\n- $U_r$ is a $m\\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $U.$\n\n- $V_r$ is a $n \\times r$ matrix obtained by keeping only the first $r$ columns of $V.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163309 :tutorial/content "Recall that the least-squares solution of the system \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf x=\\mathbf{b},\n$$\n\nis a vector ${\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}}$ such that $\\| A{\\hat{\\mathbf{x}}} - \\mathbf{b} \\|$ is minimized. \n\nThe least-squares solution is not unique when $A$ has linearly dependent columns. However, it can be shown that a so-called **optimal least-squares solution** $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}$ always exists. The optimal least-squares solution is the solution to the least squares problem with the shortest possible length.\n\nIt turns out that the optimal least-squares solution can be found using the pseudoinverse of $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x^\\ast} = A^{+} \\mathbf{b}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162228 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
